1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,808
The school asks you to explain what is normal, identify a meaningful
change and choose the next action. This rehearsal follows those
requirements using two new unfolding cases.

2
00:00:11,808 --> 00:00:24,720
Use the six earlier Week Seven lectures as tools rather than separate
diagnosis lists. The same altered patient may need neurological,
glucose, infection and pregnancy assessment together.

3
00:00:24,720 --> 00:00:37,872
The examples do not reproduce the school seventy-year-old stroke case
or its twenty-eight-year-old maternal case. Their values are assumed
training data, not patient observations or official assessment
answers.

4
00:00:37,872 --> 00:00:49,584
Practise saying what you found, what remains uncertain, what you will
do and how you will reassess. Reading alone does not replace observed
device practice or the school skill check.

5
00:00:49,584 --> 00:01:02,568
Use anatomy and physiology to explain the deficit, altered fluid
distribution or changed hormone response. The mechanism should predict
a useful observation rather than merely add terminology.

6
00:01:02,568 --> 00:01:14,784
Assessment readings explain how to obtain a reliable finding and
compare it with baseline. A value loses meaning when technique, units,
timing or the source of history is unclear.

7
00:01:14,784 --> 00:01:24,216
Drug and pathway readings connect the finding to permitted care. A
hospital treatment paragraph does not give every responder authority
to perform it.

8
00:01:24,216 --> 00:01:36,288
Mental-health and medical-surgical readings keep cognition, function,
communication and longer-term needs visible. They help prevent a
chronic diagnosis from hiding a new emergency.

9
00:01:36,288 --> 00:02:00,960
The school also links thirteen articles and one birth video outside
its eleven-row textbook table. The article guides now sit inside the
relevant maternal, neurological, toxic-renal, anaphylaxis and
endocrine lectures. They explain the assigned material alongside
deeper mechanisms and original cases, rather than treating each web
page as a separate disconnected mini-course.

10
00:02:00,960 --> 00:02:27,024
Keep source identity visible. The linked endocrine Part Two is
specifically about hypothyroidism; the private birth video has not
been reviewed, and normal birth is taught from the available article
and connected birth lessons instead. Historical treatment details are
reconciled with current local pathways. Learn why the finding matters,
then explain the assessment, action, response and limit for this
patient.

11
00:02:27,024 --> 00:03:16,440
Use the additional anatomical terms as coordinates, not disconnected
labels. The neuraxis bends between forebrain and cord, so
dorsal/ventral orientation changes with region. The midbrain tectum is
the aqueduct roof and includes colliculi; tegmentum lies ventrally and
continues into wider brainstem circuitry. Superior colliculi support
orientation, inferior colliculi auditory processing, and the medullary
inferior olive contributes motor-learning circuits. A sulcus is a
groove, a gyrus a ridge, and Brodmann areas are a microscopic cortical
map. These landmarks refine an observed eye, sensory, movement or
awareness change; they do not let a field examination prove one
damaged nucleus.

12
00:03:16,440 --> 00:04:06,288
The actual glossary contains errors that would reverse a localization
answer: precentral is frontal primary motor cortex; postcentral is
parietal primary somatosensory cortex, not the swapped definitions
printed there. Keep substantia nigra pars compacta as a dopaminergic
modulator and pars reticulata with GPi as important inhibitory output,
rather than substituting compacta in the indirect-output definition.
Paravertebral ganglia are the sympathetic chain, not a second group
superior to it; prevertebral ganglia lie anterior to the vertebral
column. Thirty-one spinal nerves means thirty-one pairs, and a lateral
white column contains sensory and motor tracts. These corrections
agree with the preceding mechanisms; they are not a new instructor
correction.

13
00:04:06,288 --> 00:04:50,208
The actual autoimmune table reverses the Graves/Hashimoto receptor
explanations. Graves typically involves stimulating TSH-receptor
antibodies and excess thyroid-hormone production; Hashimoto involves
autoimmune thyroid damage and commonly reduced production, not a
universal receptor-stimulating mechanism. Temporary excess release can
occur in an early destructive phase, so a simple high/low label is not
a complete case assessment. The original immune/endocrine teaching
above already uses the correct direction. Do not repeat the table
error on an exam explanation, but preserve which source page differs
rather than claiming the school issued a correction.

14
00:04:50,208 --> 00:05:43,992
Use mediator terms with their function, not a rigid distance rule:
chemokines guide cell migration and are a cytokine group; cytokines
coordinate responses over several spatial contexts. Histamine is one
important vasoactive mast-cell mediator, not the sole cause of every
allergy or anaphylaxis. Germinal centers support activated B-cell
responses; chyle is lipid-rich intestinal lymph and the cisterna chyli
feeds the thoracic-duct route. Graft-versus-host means donor immune
cells attack recipient tissues, unlike recipient rejection of a graft.
In an original transplant patient with confusion and rapid breathing,
connect these mechanisms to infection risk and organ assessment
without deciding that a normal temperature, one hormone history or a
familiar diagnosis explains away deterioration.

15
00:05:43,992 --> 00:05:55,824
Before the station, check the actual devices, supplies and reference
access. Confirm that the glucose display uses the units you will
report and that the pressure cuff fits the patient.

16
00:05:55,824 --> 00:06:07,104
Know who can provide advanced help and how to contact clinical
consultation. A drug name on a worksheet does not replace the actual
authorization, product or safety checks.

17
00:06:07,104 --> 00:06:20,064
For tabletop practice, describe and simulate the planned action. Use
training equipment under appropriate supervision rather than
injecting, giving medicine or doing invasive practice on another
student.

18
00:06:20,064 --> 00:06:31,320
Keep the school observed checklist and the actual service protocol
available. This course explains their reasoning but cannot certify a
device skill that has not been observed.

19
00:06:31,320 --> 00:06:42,480
Allocate tasks clearly while one person maintains overall priorities.
Glucose testing, time questions and monitoring can often proceed in
parallel with primary care.

20
00:06:42,480 --> 00:06:51,600
Use closed-loop communication for a critical task. Name the request,
have the receiver acknowledge it and report the result with units and
time.

21
00:06:51,600 --> 00:07:02,400
A partner who notices inadequate breathing or new focal weakness
should speak up immediately. Hierarchy must not hide a change while
the leader finishes the history.

22
00:07:02,400 --> 00:07:12,000
Update the working picture after important findings. Everyone should
know the immediate threat, destination concern and what will be
reassessed next.

23
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:23,352
Look for immediate airway, breathing and circulation threats before
pursuing a neat diagnosis. A patient with shallow breathing does not
wait for a complete stroke screen.

24
00:07:23,352 --> 00:07:35,688
Assess breathing depth and effectiveness as well as rate and oxygen
saturation. Supplemental oxygen may improve the displayed saturation
without correcting inadequate ventilation.

25
00:07:35,688 --> 00:07:46,728
Check glucose early when the history or function suggests an altered
state. Treat a credible low through the appropriate safe route while
continuing the rest of the assessment.

26
00:07:46,728 --> 00:07:58,416
Reassess after a move, intervention or deterioration. A completed
first pass is not proof that physiology will remain unchanged during
the rest of the encounter.

27
00:07:58,416 --> 00:08:11,088
Address the patient first and adapt to language, hearing, vision or
aphasia. Ask one question at a time and allow a response rather than
treating difficulty speaking as refusal.

28
00:08:11,088 --> 00:08:20,904
Ask a witness what was directly observed and the last normal
interaction. Separate an estimate, a second-hand report and a
documented time.

29
00:08:20,904 --> 00:08:31,344
Use medicines and relevant records to clarify risk, not to replace the
clinical picture. A drug packet cannot tell you the last swallowed
dose unless that is confirmed.

30
00:08:31,344 --> 00:08:42,528
If information remains unknown, say so in the pre-alert and record. Do
not invent a negative allergy, normal baseline or delivery date to
complete a form.

31
00:08:42,528 --> 00:08:53,784
A sixty-two-year-old has new difficulty speaking and right-arm
weakness. A companion last had a normal conversation at ten twelve,
then found the change at ten forty.

32
00:08:53,784 --> 00:09:02,520
At ten forty-eight the crew measures glucose of three point zero. The
actual onset between the two witness times is not known.

33
00:09:02,520 --> 00:09:13,320
Describe the current airway protection, breathing and perfusion rather
than assume they are normal because this looks neurological. Begin
appropriate support and early coordination.

34
00:09:13,320 --> 00:09:22,560
The low is actionable, but it does not make the focal deficit
irrelevant. Prepare to reassess the same functions after glucose
correction.

35
00:09:22,560 --> 00:09:34,656
Confirm the measured value and relevant technique, but do not create
an unnecessary delay when a credible low matches serious symptoms.
Simultaneously maintain primary support.

36
00:09:34,656 --> 00:09:43,968
A patient who cannot follow the required instructions or swallow
safely should not receive loose food, fluid or gel into an unprotected
airway.

37
00:09:43,968 --> 00:09:57,816
Use the permitted non-oral pathway when eligible, with product, dose,
access and reassessment checks. The earlier endocrine lecture explains
the current local D ten and sealed nasal glucagon choices.

38
00:09:57,816 --> 00:10:08,424
Glucose-first is not a rule to delay ventilation, bleeding control or
resource activation. It is an early diagnostic and treatment task
within the whole encounter.

39
00:10:08,424 --> 00:10:20,016
For this training branch, assume authorized glucose treatment was
completed and documented. At ten fifty-one glucose is five point six
and alertness is better.

40
00:10:20,016 --> 00:10:30,912
The right arm still drifts and the patient still cannot name familiar
objects as usual. Compare the actual repeat with the initial findings
rather than write simply improved.

41
00:10:30,912 --> 00:10:42,624
The low has been corrected, but the focal problem remains. Continue
the stroke pathway and consider other concurrent causes without
waiting for every symptom to become more dramatic.

42
00:10:42,624 --> 00:10:53,376
Even complete resolution would still need appropriate evaluation and
recurrence assessment. It would not automatically prove that no stroke
or transient vascular event occurred.

43
00:10:53,376 --> 00:11:02,304
Ten twelve is the last known well supported by this witness. Ten forty
is the discovery time, not a substituted onset.

44
00:11:02,304 --> 00:11:13,416
Write who supplied the information and whether the earlier interaction
confirmed usual speech and movement. A vague message that the patient
was probably fine is weaker evidence.

45
00:11:13,416 --> 00:11:22,992
Preserve the treatment and repeat-assessment times separately. A
receiving team needs to know both the original deficit and the state
after correction.

46
00:11:22,992 --> 00:11:32,784
Unknown onset does not remove urgency. Imaging-based selection and the
regional pathway determine the definitive options, not an invented
start time.

47
00:11:32,784 --> 00:11:43,536
In the repeat training assessment, right-arm drift remains and naming
is abnormal. Record what happens during the actual task, not what you
think the patient could do with coaching.

48
00:11:43,536 --> 00:11:56,328
Complete both FAST and VAN as the checked local tool directs. For this
branch, motor weakness with aphasia supports a positive VAN concern,
even without another abnormal cortical item.

49
00:11:56,328 --> 00:12:09,288
Separate language impairment from slurred articulation, and account
for baseline language or sensory needs. One unsuccessful English
phrase in a non-English speaker is not automatically aphasia.

50
00:12:09,288 --> 00:12:18,768
A negative screen cannot exclude every posterior, mild or atypical
stroke. Continue reasoning from the complete current presentation.

51
00:12:18,768 --> 00:12:28,440
Right-sided motor change with a language problem can fit a dominant-
hemisphere process. That is a useful explanation, not a field
angiogram.

52
00:12:28,440 --> 00:12:38,232
Do not name an exact occluded vessel, measure penumbra or rule out
bleeding from this examination. Those claims require evidence beyond a
screen.

53
00:12:38,232 --> 00:12:50,904
A seizure, migraine, toxin or systemic illness may mimic or coexist
with vascular disease. A plausible alternative should widen assessment
rather than cancel urgent care prematurely.

54
00:12:50,904 --> 00:13:00,216
Document witnessed behavior and the recovery pattern without labeling
a functional event from psychiatric history or one unusual movement.

55
00:13:00,216 --> 00:13:13,032
Provide the support the current patient needs, including indicated
oxygenation and adequate ventilation. Do not aggressively lower
pressure or give a field stroke aspirin dose from an inpatient
chapter.

56
00:13:13,032 --> 00:13:25,248
Protect the patient during movement and avoid an unsafe swallow. Do
not delay urgent conveyance for a perfect history, a second ideal IV
or repeated low-value testing.

57
00:13:25,248 --> 00:13:35,208
Use the actual regional stroke pathway with stability and available
resources. A generic comprehensive-centre label does not decide every
destination.

58
00:13:35,208 --> 00:13:47,856
Pre-notification should communicate why the team is concerned and what
has changed. The exact measured glucose and persistent deficit are
more useful than a premature definitive diagnosis.

59
00:13:47,856 --> 00:14:02,160
Here is an original teaching structure: We have a sixty-two-year-old
with persistent right-arm weakness and aphasia. Last confirmed well
was ten twelve; the change was found at ten forty and onset is
unknown.

60
00:14:02,160 --> 00:14:17,040
Glucose was three point zero at ten forty-eight and is now five point
six after authorized correction. Alertness improved, but the focal
deficit remains. Both local screens have been completed with the
findings reported.

61
00:14:17,040 --> 00:14:33,480
Then give the actual current breathing, perfusion, vital signs,
medication details, relevant anticoagulant information and estimated
arrival. In this exercise these missing values must remain unfilled
until the station supplies them.

62
00:14:33,480 --> 00:14:43,608
Use the local report format and receiving request. Do not state that a
thrombolytic is guaranteed or omit a worsening airway while reciting
the time line.

63
00:14:43,608 --> 00:14:54,960
For a second training round, change only one variable: the patient now
develops a convulsive event during conveyance. Keep the earlier times
and findings unchanged.

64
00:14:54,960 --> 00:15:04,776
Protect from injury without restraining the movement or forcing
objects into the mouth. Time the actual event and support inadequate
breathing promptly.

65
00:15:04,776 --> 00:15:17,784
Repeat glucose and consider other current threats while coordinating
the authorized advanced response. A seizure does not erase the
possibility of stroke or create ordinary PCP benzodiazepine
permission.

66
00:15:17,784 --> 00:15:28,224
Send the change and response to the receiving team. The original last-
known-well remains a historical fact even while the current management
priority changes.

67
00:15:28,224 --> 00:15:40,344
A thirty-six-year-old is sixteen days after a cesarean birth. Recovery
had been improving, but the support person reports new confusion and
increasing incisional pain and redness.

68
00:15:40,344 --> 00:15:50,064
Temperature is thirty-eight point six, pulse one hundred sixteen and
breathing twenty-four. Pressure is one hundred eighteen over seventy-
four.

69
00:15:50,064 --> 00:15:59,280
Room-air saturation is ninety-seven percent and glucose is six point
two. These two values do not explain away the new loss of function.

70
00:15:59,280 --> 00:16:09,960
This differs from the school case while rehearsing the same early-
recognition and history problems. Start with the patient rather than
wait for systolic pressure below ninety.

71
00:16:09,960 --> 00:16:22,344
Assess airway protection and effective breathing, then perfusion and
mental function. A single blood pressure within a common range does
not prove normal flow or exclude sepsis.

72
00:16:22,344 --> 00:16:33,744
Ask what the patient could do earlier today and what is different now.
New inability to follow a simple conversation is more informative than
the unqualified word confused.

73
00:16:33,744 --> 00:16:44,952
Repeat observations after care and during movement. Report the trend
and any falling responsiveness even if the original pressure has not
become dramatically low.

74
00:16:44,952 --> 00:16:56,208
Possible infection with otherwise unexplained new dysfunction warrants
early concern and urgent evaluation. The final source and diagnosis
may still be uncertain.

75
00:16:56,208 --> 00:17:08,160
In this original case, temperature, heart rate and respiratory rate
satisfy the conventional SIRS observations used by the school
exercise. The white-cell result is unknown.

76
00:17:08,160 --> 00:17:18,120
Say three observed positive domains rather than four out of four. An
unavailable laboratory result must not be assumed normal or abnormal.

77
00:17:18,120 --> 00:17:31,800
SIRS describes a systemic inflammatory pattern and can occur without
infection. Modern sepsis adds an infection-related dysfunctional host
response with organ dysfunction, so the score is not the diagnosis.

78
00:17:31,800 --> 00:17:43,056
Do not wait for a full SIRS count, fever or shock threshold when
credible deterioration is present. Maternal pathway and screening-
population limits remain important.

79
00:17:43,056 --> 00:17:55,248
Normal pregnancy adaptations do not make every postpartum abnormality
physiological. Establish the actual recovery trajectory, recent
function and delivery complications.

80
00:17:55,248 --> 00:18:07,464
The new confusion, fever and worsening wound symptoms are a meaningful
change. A prior normal prenatal pressure does not exclude a separate
postpartum hypertensive problem.

81
00:18:07,464 --> 00:18:16,368
Use the appropriate maternity escalation process. Do not apply a
generic low NEWS score as proof that this postpartum patient is safe.

82
00:18:16,368 --> 00:18:27,576
Ask about birth or pregnancy loss even when the abdomen is not
enlarged. Sensitive questions should be private, respectful and
limited to information that affects care.

83
00:18:27,576 --> 00:18:40,080
Ask about recent surgery, complications, discharge instructions and
current medicines. Clarify antibiotic or analgesic use without
assuming the medicine proves the diagnosis.

84
00:18:40,080 --> 00:18:52,392
Explore wound, pelvic, urinary, breast and respiratory symptoms as
relevant. A red incision is a possible source but does not remove the
need to assess other systems.

85
00:18:52,392 --> 00:19:05,448
Ask about bleeding, severe headache, visual change, chest symptoms and
breathlessness. These can redirect priorities toward hemorrhage,
hypertensive disease or cardiopulmonary complications.

86
00:19:05,448 --> 00:19:18,312
If confusion prevents reliable answers, obtain attributed collateral
and relevant documents while continuing care. Unknown blood loss or an
uncertain medication time stays uncertain.

87
00:19:18,312 --> 00:19:30,144
A meaningful differential explains what evidence supports the leading
problem and what dangerous alternative still needs checking. It is not
a long list spoken after the decision is already fixed.

88
00:19:30,144 --> 00:19:42,840
Infection is plausible here, but active bleeding, hypertensive
neurological disease, thromboembolism, cardiomyopathy or a metabolic
problem may coexist or better explain new findings.

89
00:19:42,840 --> 00:19:58,152
The school mentions ectopic pregnancy as a safety check. Do not
mechanically force it onto a verified sixteen-day postpartum history;
clarify whether the reported pregnancy event was actually a birth,
current pregnancy or loss.

90
00:19:58,152 --> 00:20:08,688
For each relevant branch identify one useful observation and the
action it would change. Then return to current breathing, perfusion
and the transport plan.

91
00:20:08,688 --> 00:20:21,168
In the next practice round, add one fact: the support person reports
fresh heavier vaginal bleeding while the crew prepares to move. Do not
rewrite the earlier wound or fever findings.

92
00:20:21,168 --> 00:20:32,040
Clarify the real amount and timing and assess current perfusion. A pad
description is useful history but does not measure every concealed or
previous blood loss.

93
00:20:32,040 --> 00:20:43,848
Infection and hemorrhage can coexist. Do not demand that a patient fit
only one station diagnosis before using the appropriate maternal
escalation and urgent conveyance.

94
00:20:43,848 --> 00:20:55,416
Update the receiving team with the new fact and current response. Do
not stand an unwell patient to provoke orthostatic symptoms or perform
an unauthorized examination.

95
00:20:55,416 --> 00:21:07,392
Support the current physiological threat and reassess frequently. A
high oxygen number does not replace breathing assessment, and a normal
glucose does not remove a neurological problem.

96
00:21:07,392 --> 00:21:20,520
Use the actual authorized fluid and medication plan, assessing
response and overload risk. Do not copy a large inpatient sepsis total
or specialist obstetric regimen into a PCP station.

97
00:21:20,520 --> 00:21:32,256
Arrange urgent evaluation and early notification without waiting for
shock. Antibiotics and source control require the appropriate clinical
team and established program.

98
00:21:32,256 --> 00:21:43,752
Explain the concern to the patient and support person in clear
language. Preserve appropriate privacy, comfort, thermal care and
dignity while organizing movement.

99
00:21:43,752 --> 00:21:58,344
An original teaching opening is: We have a thirty-six-year-old sixteen
days after cesarean delivery, with new confusion and worsening
incisional pain and redness. We are concerned about infection-
associated deterioration.

100
00:21:58,344 --> 00:22:11,256
Give the measured temperature, pulse, breathing rate, pressure, oxygen
context and glucose with times. State that the pressure is currently
preserved rather than call the patient stable.

101
00:22:11,256 --> 00:22:24,360
Report any new bleeding, relevant negative findings only when actually
assessed, and history gaps that may affect the receiving plan. Do not
invent normal uterine findings or a negative clot history.

102
00:22:24,360 --> 00:22:38,496
Close with support provided, response, additional help, actual
destination and estimated arrival. Ask for the appropriate receiving
or consultation plan rather than promise a final diagnosis.

103
00:22:38,496 --> 00:22:50,568
A patient who becomes quieter may be deteriorating rather than
settling. Repeat attention, response and breathing instead of assuming
that less agitation means improvement.

104
00:22:50,568 --> 00:23:03,216
The PMHN reading distinguishes delirium from chronic cognitive
impairment. Acute fluctuating inattention can coexist with dementia,
medical illness or a psychiatric diagnosis.

105
00:23:03,216 --> 00:23:16,656
Use a calm environment, sensory aids and simple explanations while
assessing causes. De-escalation is compatible with urgent medical care
and does not justify leaving the person unobserved.

106
00:23:16,656 --> 00:23:27,864
Capacity is decision-specific and time-specific. A diagnosis, low
cognitive score or relative presence does not automatically authorize
someone else to decide.

107
00:23:27,864 --> 00:23:38,232
After an intervention, state what actually changed. Glucose rose,
breathing became deeper or the patient could answer a question are
different observations.

108
00:23:38,232 --> 00:23:48,240
Then compare with the desired effect. A better number without restored
function may indicate incomplete response, recurrence or a second
problem.

109
00:23:48,240 --> 00:24:02,400
Actively look for harm, including inadequate ventilation, worsening
perfusion, extravasation, overload or new symptoms relevant to the
intervention. Do not document no adverse effect without checking.

110
00:24:02,400 --> 00:24:11,784
Use the findings to choose the next action and communicate it.
Repeating the original treatment simply because it was first chosen is
not reassessment.

111
00:24:11,784 --> 00:24:23,448
Use the applicable local transfer format and identify what is new
since the pre-alert. The receiving clinician needs the current
patient, not only the initial chief complaint.

112
00:24:23,448 --> 00:24:35,208
Give actual measurements with units and times, including oxygen
conditions and repeat neurological findings. Separate reported blood
loss or symptoms from measured observations.

113
00:24:35,208 --> 00:24:49,920
Medication details include the actual product, dose, route, time and
observed response, plus relevant prior patient or caregiver doses. Do
not omit a failed access or treatment problem that affects the next
team.

114
00:24:49,920 --> 00:25:00,360
Preserve unknowns and the source of collateral. Complete the record
from the actual encounter rather than reconstruct a perfect-looking
story after the event.

115
00:25:00,360 --> 00:25:09,144
Run the first round from scene approach to pre-alert using only the
initial facts. Speak your priorities and ask for the observations you
need.

116
00:25:09,144 --> 00:25:19,968
In round two add one finding, such as persistent aphasia after glucose
correction or new bleeding in the postpartum case. Explain how it
changes the working plan.

117
00:25:19,968 --> 00:25:30,984
In round three introduce an intervention response or deterioration.
Assess it before the facilitator offers another diagnosis, so the
reasoning remains visible.

118
00:25:30,984 --> 00:25:41,088
Repeat the missed step rather than restart a different unrelated case.
The aim is a reliable clinical sequence, not memorizing one ideal
script.

119
00:25:41,088 --> 00:25:52,824
Give feedback about what was observed: the crew maintained ventilation
while a partner checked glucose, or the report preserved an unknown
onset. Avoid vague good or bad labels.

120
00:25:52,824 --> 00:26:05,424
For the maternal case, check that abnormal function was recognized
before shock and that SIRS was counted honestly. A correct infection
label does not excuse an unsafe swallow or ignored bleeding.

121
00:26:05,424 --> 00:26:15,744
Choose one measurable improvement, such as repeating focal findings
after treatment or stating the source of the last-known-well time.
Then repeat that moment.

122
00:26:15,744 --> 00:26:26,544
Separate a tabletop success from observed equipment competence or
official school acceptance. Record what was practised and what still
needs instructor observation.

123
00:26:26,544 --> 00:26:36,096
You should be able to connect a new symptom to the relevant normal
physiology and explain at least one dangerous alternative without
losing the immediate priority.

124
00:26:36,096 --> 00:26:46,704
Demonstrate the assessment sequence using the actual school tools and
supervised equipment. State the operator and consultation boundaries
for the proposed treatment.

125
00:26:46,704 --> 00:26:58,368
After every meaningful change, reassess function and physiology and
communicate the effect. A correct initial diagnosis is not enough when
the patient evolves.

126
00:26:58,368 --> 00:27:08,784
Use the bilingual assigned-reading guides to close the mechanism and
terminology gaps, then retest the original case with new data rather
than memorize the school assessment.
